\section{What it installs}{


The \strong{required} packages come with the package itself, so on a normal
install there is nothing to do — this only matters if the folder was copied
rather than installed.

The \strong{optional} ones are the point. \code{srvyr}, \code{analysistools} and
\code{cleaningtools} are in \code{Suggests} and are \emph{not} installed with the package,
and two of them are not on CRAN at all. Run this once and the survey engine
works:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{load_packages()
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\strong{You do not need them for most runs.} An \code{analysis} row with a \code{level} set
asks for a confidence interval, which routes that row through \code{srvyr} and
\code{analysistools}. Leave every \code{level} cell empty and the fast tabulation
engine handles the whole run, needing none of the three — and on a large
disaggregation taking seconds rather than minutes. So if \code{load_packages()}
cannot reach GitHub, that is inconvenient rather than fatal.
}

\section{Why it does not attach by default}{


Unlike a folder of scripts, this is a package: every internal call is
namespace-qualified (\code{dplyr::bind_rows()}, \code{openxlsx::createStyle()}), so
nothing needs to be on your search path for the analysis to run. Attaching
\code{dplyr} would also mask \code{stats::filter()} and \code{stats::lag()} in your session
for no gain. Pass \code{attach = TRUE} if you want them anyway, for your own
interactive work.
}

